[Music]  on the ground as troops fight bombs fall  and people flee from the terror this  looks very much like a war between  ukraine and russian invaders  but something else is happening here too  for while europe and the us have refused  to fight militarily or impose a no-fly  zone they are already at economic war  with russia  we have purposely designed these  sanctions to maximize the long-term  impact on russia these sanctions will  suppress russia's economic growth we  will continue on a remorseless mission  to squeeze russia from the global  economy piece by piece  this  is a financial battle one that could  change the very nature of the world  economy an invisible assault that goes  further than anything we've ever seen  before  this is something we did not do in world  war ii with germany that really is a new  use of financial power that's quite  unprecedented i'm definitely worried yes  it's worth just taking a step back and  considering the scale and the speed of  what we're talking about here in terms  of sanctions from america and europe  you've got financial sanctions on  individuals oligarchs politicians we've  had them before but they've gone even  further you've got the financial  messaging network swift russia's been  cut off from it it makes it even more  difficult to get money in and out and  then you've got trades new tariffs on  goods coming out of russia effectively  it is a pariah in the global trading  system and all of this has happened very  quickly because when it comes to  economic war  speed matters  this was an attempt by europe and the  united states to tilt the battlefield in  ukraine's favor and i still think to a  degree that is the way in which we're  thinking about these measures is a sort  of siege against russia a counter siege  of an economic financial trade tech  variety to try and alter the outcome in  military terms of a struggle which is  not resolved  and the list of measures go on we've  seen companies going beyond the rules  pulling out of russia even when they  don't need to like mcdonald's and visa  and mercedes there's a ban on selling  any product of russia with american  technology in it which means it's  essentially cut off from the most  advanced computer tech  and then there's the final punishment  perhaps the biggest which comes down to  what many in russia saw as their  financial trump card reserves  reserves are essentially a pile of money  or gold that countries set aside as an  insurance policy and russia has an awful  lot of them it's supposed to buy you  time in the event of a financial crisis  but here's the thing a lot of those  reserves are not in moscow but actually  in european countries in paris and in  frankfurt and in london and when these  financial sanctions were imposed europe  and the u.s said no you don't  we're gonna  cut you off  i don't think that anyone has frozen  central bank reserves in the way that  western countries have with russia's  reserves and so that really is a new  use of financial power that's quite  unprecedented  the upshot in the following days a  collapse in the russian currency the  ruble it's unclear whether russia will  be able to pay any of its foreign  currency debt in the coming months not  because it doesn't have the money  because it's not allowed to use the  money a default but not as we know it  that brings us to the impact and in  short it could be very bad for russia a  cataclysmic recession perhaps even worse  than in ukraine raising the question was  that the plan if this is economic war  what constitutes victory  the battle could be we want to weaken  the russian economy to the point that  it's either unable to be a danger and  or um  keep making sure that it's unable to  perform these actions again in the  future so there is indeed a war logic  being applied  the impact elsewhere around the world  will also be severe we could see a food  crisis especially in sub-saharan africa  which is enormously reliant on russian  and ukrainian wheat  russia and ukraine altogether account  for about 30 percent of wheat export as  you know fertilizers as well so  cutting this production off for a number  of countries will have two impacts one  is that we are seeing global pricings  rising  uh and the other one is that some of the  sweets and some countries really needed  it badly and then there's energy russia  has the world's biggest reserves of  natural gas europe massively reliant on  it and you might have thought that would  have gone down since the invasion but  have a look at this  actually since the invasion flows of  natural gas from russia to europe have  gone up to the highest level so far this  year in other words even as europe  imposes sanctions on russia it is also  funneling billions of euros of cash  across to russia to finance energy  if you're involved in a long protracted  siege of russia you have to pace  yourself i mean this is the position of  the german government right we precisely  because we're serious about this we need  to move in a deliberate fashion  in other words there is real politic  pragmatism going on here even amid the  economic war but for germany and the  rest this conflict is likely to do  something else redraw the map  for decades germany practiced aust  politic trying to draw russia closer to  europe through economic and energy ties  but now in the face of economic war does  russia lean to the east towards india  towards china and are western nations  redouble their alliance and does all of  that constitute a new cold war i do  think the world is going to be different  because  i think that the degree of risk that  people have  recognized  a strategic risk that exists in the  world is not going to go away  even if in the short run the russians  are forced to to back down  and that has a lot of consequences for  the way we regard globalized trade and  our dependence on other countries  in other words this may only be the  start of a bigger economic war with  bigger consequences  globalization the way the world fits  together has long been at a crossroads  is this the moment it heads down a  different path altogether  you

